WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.502
We successfully implemented the first feature.

2
00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:04.771
Can't wait to do it again and again.

3
00:00:05.472 --> 00:00:07.774
Don't rush into it. Take a step back

4
00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:11.277
and reflect with some replanning.

5
00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:14.080
You have to run slow to run fast.

6
00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:17.283
We have a workflow step called validation.

7
00:00:17.884 --> 00:00:20.186
Tests are good for validation, but we

8
00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:22.789
didn't give the agent all our testing preferences.

9
00:00:23.056 --> 00:00:25.658
Let's make a replanning branch

10
00:00:25.759 --> 00:00:28.361
to update the tech stack in our constitution.

11
00:00:28.962 --> 00:00:30.864
As you saw in the previous video,

12
00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:33.433
spec versioning is an evolving topic.

13
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:35.635
The constitution is a living document.

14
00:00:35.935 --> 00:00:37.804
It's good practice to make updates

15
00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:39.939
to it in its own separate branch,

16
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:41.941
so you can keep track of

17
00:00:41.941 --> 00:00:43.943
which versions of it produced which code.

18
00:00:44.944 --> 00:00:47.147
We'll use a prompt to state our policy.

19
00:00:51.418 --> 00:00:54.020
It looks like the agent updated the package.json

20
00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:57.023
just to have a script, no dependency.

21
00:00:57.323 --> 00:01:00.326
Something to note for later, when we do run tests.

22
00:01:00.794 --> 00:01:03.396
This change to add a testing framework

23
00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.265
applies to future code.

24
00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:08.568
But let's tell the agent to update existing

25
00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:10.570
feature specs and implementation

26
00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.872
based on this constitution change.

27
00:01:16.443 --> 00:01:19.446
After these two prompts, we have some good changes.

28
00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:22.148
The agent set up this testing change,

29
00:01:22.449 --> 00:01:24.451
but didn't write the tests themselves.

30
00:01:24.951 --> 00:01:27.420
Let's tell the agent to write some new tests.

31
00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:37.931
With this setup, we can run tests conveniently in our editor.

32
00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:41.434
Let's run under the debugger to give us a chance

33
00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:43.736
to step through code execution as

34
00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:46.339
part of the human in the loop.

35
00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:51.544
Looks good. We now have tests as part of validation.

36
00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:53.746
Let's commit.

37
00:01:58.151 --> 00:01:59.986
Sometimes we realize we want to

38
00:01:59.986 --> 00:02:02.155
do something differently in the product plan.

39
00:02:02.856 --> 00:02:05.859
For example, after implementing the first feature,

40
00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:08.228
we got an update from the product manager.

41
00:02:08.528 --> 00:02:11.464
Because 40% of our users are on mobile,

42
00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:14.167
we want to emphasize a responsive design.

43
00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:16.603
Let's go to the agent.

44
00:02:16.803 --> 00:02:18.805
Tell the agent we want responsive design

45
00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:21.307
and to correct the product specs and

46
00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:23.409
feature specs as well as any code.

47
00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.412
Since we're so early on in development,

48
00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:29.415
this update is a small change, so it makes sense

49
00:02:29.415 --> 00:02:31.818
to directly implement it during replanning.

50
00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:35.622
But if the new work is big, it's better to schedule it

51
00:02:35.622 --> 00:02:38.424
on the roadmap as its own feature phase,

52
00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:42.428
instead of just doing it in replanning. Use your judgment.

53
00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:47.133
Remember, we want the specs to capture decisions, not just the code.

54
00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:49.936
We want to try to keep

55
00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:52.138
both in sync to help team communication.

56
00:02:56.409 --> 00:02:58.778
The agent has implemented our responsive fix

57
00:02:59.379 --> 00:03:01.381
with updates not just in code,

58
00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:04.083
but also to the product and feature specs.

59
00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:07.921
Time for us to do our part and review the changes.

60
00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:12.292
Let's go through the diffs. This looks good. Let's do a commit.

61
00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:14.794
Remember, working in small steps

62
00:03:14.994 --> 00:03:16.996
with frequent commits keeps the review

63
00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:19.599
from overloading your brain.

64
00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:22.936
While this first part of replanning is working,

65
00:03:22.936 --> 00:03:25.638
let's step back and do some project housekeeping.

66
00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:28.942
For example, let's revisit the project roadmap

67
00:03:28.942 --> 00:03:30.910
and look at the next task.

68
00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.912
Does it still make sense?

69
00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:34.781
The roadmap shows the next feature

70
00:03:34.781 --> 00:03:36.716
which we cover in the next lesson.

71
00:03:37.217 --> 00:03:39.219
Is it still the correct one?

72
00:03:39.619 --> 00:03:42.622
Looking through the roadmap, it looks like features two, three,

73
00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:45.325
four, and five kind of hang together.

74
00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:49.495
Let's update the roadmap to tackle those in one step.

75
00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:53.299
We'll commit and start on this new feature shortly.

76
00:03:54.701 --> 00:03:58.171
The replanning step can be about a feature or the whole project.

77
00:03:58.972 --> 00:04:03.972
But replanning can also be about improving your spec-driven development workflow,

78
00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:07.680
across projects, across your organization.

79
00:04:08.314 --> 00:04:12.318
For example, maybe you have a few non-technical stakeholders

80
00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:15.021
that want to monitor the project's progress.

81
00:04:15.822 --> 00:04:18.324
You'd like it to update a changelog

82
00:04:18.725 --> 00:04:20.326
on each merge to main.

83
00:04:20.727 --> 00:04:23.329
Most AI coding agents support skills,

84
00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:25.732
a package of instructions and resources

85
00:04:25.732 --> 00:04:29.535
providing the agent new capabilities and expertise.

86
00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:33.640
Skills are great for definable, repeatable workflows

87
00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:38.344
that require context specific to your project or organization.

88
00:04:38.745 --> 00:04:42.548
A skill would be great for implementing a changelog update.

89
00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:45.551
You could write this skill by hand,

90
00:04:45.652 --> 00:04:48.254
but many agents actually have skills

91
00:04:48.354 --> 00:04:50.156
that help you write a skill.

92
00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:53.426
Let's use the agent to help us write

93
00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:55.228
and maintain a changelog skill.

94
00:04:57.797 --> 00:04:59.766
The agent gets to work.

95
00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:02.969
One thing for us to consider is

96
00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:05.371
this skill unique to this project?

97
00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:08.975
Or should it be a standard part of all projects?

98
00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:11.778
This is a style choice and you'll

99
00:05:11.778 --> 00:05:14.047
gain better understanding as you use it.

100
00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:17.517
You'll learn to automate your SDD workflow with skills.

101
00:05:18.117 --> 00:05:22.488
For example, your validation step might include updating the readme,

102
00:05:22.822 --> 00:05:25.725
linting, formatting, test writing,

103
00:05:26.426 --> 00:05:28.394
and other quality checks.

104
00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:30.697
You can work with your agent to package those

105
00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:32.899
into a validation skill.

106
00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:36.402
Repeatable process, less manual work.

107
00:05:36.936 --> 00:05:39.038
The agent is finished.

108
00:05:39.038 --> 00:05:40.873
We can scroll through the window to see

109
00:05:40.873 --> 00:05:42.709
what it created and how to use it.

110
00:05:43.409 --> 00:05:45.411
Note that it chose to create this

111
00:05:45.411 --> 00:05:47.413
in the global skills area.

112
00:05:47.847 --> 00:05:51.250
This skill will now be usable across all projects.

113
00:05:52.251 --> 00:05:54.554
Also, Claude Code used the new skill

114
00:05:54.554 --> 00:05:57.256
to generate a CHANGELOG in this project.

115
00:05:57.957 --> 00:06:01.761
CHANGELOGs are how you talk to your stakeholders, including the agent.

116
00:06:02.462 --> 00:06:04.864
So, let's open it and review it manually.

117
00:06:05.298 --> 00:06:09.102
Looks good. We are finished with this replanning work.

118
00:06:09.502 --> 00:06:11.504
Let's wrap up with a commit.

119
00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:17.810
Then merge the branch.

120
00:06:18.511 --> 00:06:21.481
Now that most of your work as a

121
00:06:21.481 --> 00:06:24.817
developer is in planning and validation, rather than implementing,

122
00:06:25.218 --> 00:06:28.321
make time between features to replan.

123
00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:32.091
In the next video, we'll work on another roadmap feature.
